explanation:
The theorem says that pushing an additive Haar measure on the completion `adicCompletion ℚ p` forward along `x ↦ u * x` gives `(modulus u)⁻¹ • ν`. Here `modulus` is the library's own definition.

I read `Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Haar/DistribChar.lean`. It has:
- `distribHaarChar`, the definition;
- `distribHaarChar_pos`;
- `addHaarScalarFactor_smul_eq_distribHaarChar` and its `_inv` variants;
- `distribHaarChar_mul`, which says `Δ(g) * μ s = μ (g • s)`;
- `distribHaarChar_eq_div` and `distribHaarChar_eq_of_measure_smul_eq_mul`.

None of these is stated as a pushforward `Measure.map`. The closest is `addHaarScalarFactor_smul_eq_distribHaarChar_inv`, which is phrased with `DomMulAct` and `addHaarScalarFactor`, not with `map`. I also checked `ModularCharacter.lean`, whose `map_right_mul_eq_modularCharacterFun_smul` covers multiplicative right translation in a group, not the distributive action on an additive group.

The library's proof uses `distribHaarChar_mul` together with a library lemma relating `modulus` to `distribHaarChar` (`modulus_coe_units`) and a preimage rewrite (`smul_set_eq_preimage`). That is more than routine glue around a single Mathlib declaration, so I found no Mathlib result that states this directly or gives it as a special case.
